joe biden is in europe this week to try  to demonstrate unity with european  allies over russia's invasion of ukraine  so we've come to the bundestag the  german parliament here in berlin to  speak to germany's transatlantic  coordinator man called michel link i  want to find out from him  just how united the allies really are  and whether germany is really pulling  its weight michelle let's start with  germany it's been going through this  huge transformation of its foreign and  security policy just in the last few  weeks since the war began  spending suddenly 100 billion extra  euros on defense  how important is this moment for germany  it's an important moment for germany but  it's also a watershed moment for the  european union it's not only germany  spending more it's the european union  for the first time setting up  a program a massive program where the  united  well sorry where the european union is  purchasing weapons it's delivering  weapons  is even having a  ready-made standing force  in the future in the strategic compass  so it's a watershed moment for berlin  but also for our union so some people  around the world used to look at germany  and sort of think of it as a sort of a  big switzerland you know a country that  like to make money and kind of keep its  its hands relatively clean  is that decisively over with now that  time  well that's in within our hands it  should be over it should definitely be  over  and it should have never been happened  in the first place because we cannot be  a large switzerland we are  as germany especially after the second  world war after our experiences we  cannot be neutral we are part of an  alliance we are part of nato we have  part of the european union  we have even a military assistance  clause in the european union treaty not  only in the nato treaty so no to any  neutrality no to any form of in between  of germany we are clearly standing as  ally  among allies and with our allies  now you know ukraine and russia very  well you know eastern europe very well  how worried are you about the situation  right now  extremely so and not only since this  brutal war which is a flagrant violation  of international law but if we can if we  tried to read the signs and the signals  and tried to understand it  already in the last years  since the very prominent and  well in famous speech of president putin  at the munich security conference  in 2007 we should have been knowing that  something is coming  maybe not openly violent and aggressive  but certainly in form of a hybrid  attack  or even war on our western structures  and especially on the unity and  of nato and the european union so it's  not new it became only more visible with  this attack  now  in your career you've had a strong focus  on democratic standards and human rights  when you look at vladimir putin when you  speak about that the speech going back  to 2007 when you look at the development  of him as a leader in russia over these  20 years but particularly in the last  few months  some people see  someone who turned into an authoritarian  but now even going beyond that sort of  almost totalitarian even sort of fascist  overtones in launching the war in the  kind of speeches the words he's been  using recently in public  is that how you see it is that what  we're dealing with here a fascist  dictatorship  i would i wouldn't try to use any  word like fascism it is for sure a  tendency of a increasingly paranoid  dictatorial structure  of its own  but it's clearly  more and more directed to one person  let's not forget he's ruling the country  since 1999.  in the beginning of course he was not as  strong as he is today and even today we  don't know exactly how the division of  labor really works in the kremlin but  one of the things is sure he pulls the  strings he's calling  really  the shots and  he is relying on a small group of people  some of them may know some we don't know  and with this small group he's steering  the country in a more and more  dictatorial way  when i had the privilege to observe the  elections in russia as chief observer in  the last presidential elections we  clearly said  there is an increasing grip on civil  society  and increasing grip also on controlling  the neighbors that could be seen already  four years ago now  nato allies in the region are of course  very anxious about what is happening  how concerned are you that russia and  the vladimir putin is going to start  really testing nato's commitment to  defend those allies  you mentioned just a moment ago that  that he's challenging the west  how concerned are you that this could  could become something where he really  tries to  challenge the very ability of nato to  protect its allies i don't want to  speculate but i'm pretty sure that in  nato we have all the scenarios ready  made in case there is such an attack on  nato territory that's at least what the  planners in nato and the defense  ministers are very good at  so there are the scenarios we don't  speak about that a lot but for sure he  will be testing and he already did  uh in more visible ways in less visible  ways in cyber attack openly and hidden  and i'm pretty sure  with the war in ukraine there will be  attempts of retaliation from the russian  side  also directed to nato and its members  since he considers our sanctions  and the delivery of weapons to ukraine  as  a  extremely unfriendly act so we should be  better prepared for it but cool-blooded  well-prepared and then if necessary act  and react very resolutely now more and  more countries particularly in eastern  europe are saying that in order to try  to stop putin sooner there does need to  be an embargo on importing russian  energy germany so far has been adamant  that it simply cannot do that it would  damage its economy too much do you think  at some point germany is going to have  to consider that and relatively soon to  try to sort of cut off the flow of money  to putin  i think it's very important to explain  that germany does not want to continue  to purchase energy in russia it is  simply not possible to stop it overnight  whether we call it embargo or stopping  the purchase we should do it as soon as  possible i'm very glad the chancellor  put it today in his in his uh  a very important speech to the bundestag  today the first after the watershed  moment he said we should end energy  purchase in russia as soon as possible i  think that's the way forward as soon as  possible it's not possible overnight by  the way we are not the only ones look to  italy look  even to some eastern european countries  we cannot end it overnight but we should  do it  as soon as possible because he  really makes use of this money in order  to run his army it isn't the point that  it might be possible but it would just  be expensive and germany doesn't want to  pay that price it will certainly cost  the price and it already does now  what we try to find is a way  to get  soon rid of any energy purchase from  russia  by first a buying elsewhere second  certainly it will be also more expensive  we will we have to face that lng  terminals cannot be built overnight so  it is a combination of being ready to  pay more but especially also to buy  elsewhere and  you cannot you cannot act in a way that  you  weaken yourself in an extreme way so  that we are not able any more than with  our economic power than to help ukraine  so when we act we should act as fast as  possible we should certainly also pay a  price for it we are ready to do that but  nevertheless it's still not possible  overnight  let's look on the european level again  and and the transatlantic factor in  defense so europe has been saying for a  long time this is a lot more urgency now  that europe has to do more for its own  defense  but aren't we seeing in this crisis just  how crucial the united states is to  european defense  if we imagine where we would be standing  if the united states was not an ally  standing at europe's side at the moment  i would rather not imagine i wouldn't  like to imagine where we would stand as  europeans without  the united states  and canada by the way but especially  united states i'm coming from south by  of germany from baden-wurttemberg  for us south germans it was always  normal to have u.s troops in germany  that was normal that was important that  was part of our life and i think we  didn't really value that contribution of  the united states to european security  and we now have to learn finally that we  have to pay a higher share in this in  sharing of that burden by the way it was  obama in 2014 at the wales summit  who really  made us clearly and in no uncertain  terms  except  the 2 percent clause  it was already way before before we had  president trump  and today it is still not fulfilled so  it's a homework to do we have assumed  that and understood  that duty and that task and we will do  it  now of course there is currently a  president in place in the white house  who looks favorably on europe who's  really tried to rebuild the the  transatlantic alliance um but it's  certainly conceivable  that a donald trump or another donald  trump-like politician could  win the presidency next time in just  over two years  how much is that an a worry for you and  your role but also for germany and  europe when it thinks about these  questions  well first of all it's  a vibrant democracy in the united states  it's a democracy that makes their own  the u.s make their own choice  but we hope that whoever is being  elected  next president but before that we have  the midterms so we are looking way ahead  now but independent of who is president  we want a waterproof relation  where we as  our side from the ocean where we carry  our responsibilities the best investment  to in good relations with the us is that  we do our job and then of course only if  we do our job we can only clearly  address the u.s side in case we don't  like anything we hear from the white  house a good friendship is not about  being quiet and one top one down it's  about everybody doing his job and also  to be open and frank when necessary i  mean with your election observer  background when you look at the  situation in electoral politics in the  united states for instance accusations  that the republic parties the republican  party is trying to  hinder voting rights especially for  minorities does this set off the  election observer alarm bells with you  that the the home of democracy is in big  democratic trouble  well i had the privilege to serve uh for  for many years as the head of the odihr  that is the main electoral watchdog and  the your viewers probably know it  whenever you have international  observers the guys with the osce badge  they are the ones whom i had the  privilege to  to to chair  also in the u.s elections in general  elections and what we are seeing there  also the proposals now on the table  partially they are just modernizing  partially they are okay  but there is a certain limit  what should not be done and that is  always when you make voting more  difficult  but again it's not our job from the  outside to lecture anybody but there are  international standards there are  international standards for good  democratic and fair elections  and they require  every participating state in the osce  including the u.s  to  make voting as easy as possible  fundamental rights should be easy to  execute easy to to use therefore i hope  very much that also in the united states  voting will not be made more complicated  but less complicated  michelle thank you very much for your  time speaking for dw thank you  you

